import CslibFrontier.Engineering.Electrical.LinearCircuit
import CslibFrontier.Algorithms.LinearAlgebra.SparseLyapunovCertificate

/-!
# Benchmark: certified stability of a fourth-order Sallen–Key low-pass filter

The circuit is two cascaded unity-gain Sallen–Key low-pass stages with E24 values (10 kΩ, 4.7 kΩ,
100 nF, 47 nF, 22 nF), each op-amp a one-pole macromodel (a 10 mS VCCS into 10 MΩ ∥ 22 pF, so
`A₀ = 10⁵`, followed by a unity VCVS buffer), a 10 µH input inductor and a 10 kΩ load: 10 nodes,
19 branches, 29 unknowns and 7 differential states, with component values from `2.2·10⁻¹¹` to
`10⁷`, all exact rationals.

The certificates were produced by an untrusted exact generator: an index-one decoupling by row
reduction and a Schur complement, the operating point, the exact solution of `AᵀP + PA = -1`
rounded to 16 significant bits per entry, exact dense `LDLᵀ` witnesses for `P` and the residual
the checker recomputes, and sparse envelope witnesses rounded to dyadics with an exact diagonally
dominant remainder. Each stage is checked by `decide +kernel` under the profiler, and the two
stability theorems (through the dense and through the sparse Lyapunov checker) end the chain.
Run it with

```
lake env lean scripts/CircuitMNABench.lean
```

It is not part of the test suite: the decoupling check takes about 13 s of kernel time and 5 GiB.
-/

open Matrix Cslib.LinearAlgebra Cslib.Electrical

set_option maxRecDepth 100000
set_option maxHeartbeats 0 -- the decoupling check takes about 13 s of kernel time

namespace CircuitMNABench

/-- Two cascaded unity-gain Sallen–Key low-pass stages (4th order), op-amps as one-pole
macromodels (gm = 10 mS, 10 MΩ, 22 pF, A0 = 1e5) with unity VCVS output buffers, a 10 µH input
inductor and a 10 kΩ load. -/
